= Consistency (logic) — Technical =

== Syntactic definitions ==
A classical theory T can be defined as consistent when:

<pre>
not exists φ such that T ⊢ φ and T ⊢ ¬φ
</pre>

or equivalently, under explosive calculi:

<pre>
not for every ψ: T ⊢ ψ
</pre>

The equivalence can fail in nonclassical systems.

== Satisfiability ==
Semantic satisfiability means:

<pre>
there exists model M such that M ⊨ T
</pre>

Soundness gives satisfiable ⇒ consistent.

Completeness of classical first-order logic gives consistent ⇒ satisfiable.

== Relative consistency ==
A model construction can show:

<pre>
Con(B) ⇒ Con(A)
</pre>

rather than prove consistency in an absolute meta-theoretic sense.

== Gödel limitation ==
For sufficiently expressive, recursively axiomatized, consistent theories containing arithmetic, Gödel's second incompleteness theorem limits internal proofs of their own consistency.

